                <pb facs="1233" n="947" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg947"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/2512</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gerard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-05-04T17:00:00+02:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 4, 1916, 5 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received May 5, 6 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3843. Your 2956, May 1, 4 p. m. Search of records here shows no correspondence
                    relative to hospital supplies in transit subsequent to Department’s reply dated
                    November 10 to my despatch 1673.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1266-f1"/> Foreign Office
                    states verbally no reply received to note sent Spanish Ambassador on October 15
                    and nothing known of any statement made by British Government last November.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gerard</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
